\begin{abstract}
Reusing a driving scene under new camera and actor states exposes surfaces that were never recorded. Reconstructing such a scene requires recovering the observed content, completing missing geometry and appearance, and retaining a stable identity for each movable component. We present DriveCompose, a framework for generative reconstruction of compositional driving scenes from calibrated image sequences. It jointly completes a world-space background and canonical vehicle and human assets, so generated surfaces inherit the coordinate frames and motion bindings used for later scene editing. A conditional flow model combines local attention with cross-component memory, while observed codes, known free space, and input-view checks constrain the completed Gaussian scene. On Waymo, DriveCompose reduces FID from 83.72 to 37.43 relative to STORM for $\pm2\,\mathrm{m}$ camera shifts, and reaches 26.47 dB PSNR in zero-shot transfer to nuScenes. Cross-component memory lowers unseen-surface RGB error by 20.5\% relative to isolated per-component memories with eight input timestamps. The resulting scene can be rendered under different camera and actor states without resampling its completed geometry or appearance.
