\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Driving-scene reconstruction.}
NeRF and Gaussian splatting provide the radiance representations underlying urban reconstruction~\cite{mildenhall2020nerf,kerbl2023gaussians}, with driving-specific rendering developed in NeuRAD and SplatAD~\cite{tonderski2024neurad,hess2025splatad}. Feed-forward methods learn geometry and Gaussian prediction from images~\cite{hong2024lrm,charatan2024pixelsplat,chen2024mvsplat,wang2024dust3r,wang2025vggt}. For driving logs, STORM aggregates dynamic Gaussians through scene flow, while DGGT combines camera recovery with temporal visibility and motion-guided interpolation~\cite{yang2025storm,chen2025dggt}. DrivingForward, StreetForward, PointForward, and Instant NuRec further explore surround-view inputs, temporal processing, point-aligned representations, and simulation-oriented reconstruction~\cite{tian2025drivingforward,yu2026streetforward,chi2026pointforward,huang2026instantnurec}. TokenGS also decouples Gaussian prediction from pixels through learnable tokens~\cite{ren2026tokengs}. DriveCompose aggregates recovered evidence onto background and canonical asset supports, then conditionally generates their missing content before Gaussian decoding.

\paragraph{Compositional scenes and controllable assets.}
Neural Scene Graphs, Street Gaussians, and DrivingGaussian separate scene content into background and moving components~\cite{ost2021neuralgraphs,yan2024street,zhou2024drivinggaussian}. OmniRe includes canonical representations for rigid and articulated actors, while UniSim and HUGSIM connect reconstructed content to closed-loop simulation~\cite{chen2025omnire,yang2023unisim,zhou2024hugsim}. At the asset level, SMPL-based body models and animatable Gaussian methods provide canonical surfaces and motion bindings~\cite{loper2015smpl,pavlakos2019smplx,hu2024gauhuman,kocabas2024hugs,qian2024avatar}; LHM and IDOL infer animatable humans, and UrbanCAD targets controllable vehicles~\cite{qiu2025lhm,zhuang2025idol,lu2025urbancad}. These works establish the representation and control mechanisms used to manipulate scene components. Our completion variables retain this component structure, so generated surfaces inherit an asset identity, coordinate frame, and motion binding.

\paragraph{Generative reconstruction and completion.}
Image-conditioned 3D generation uses generated views~\cite{liu2023zero123,shi2024mvdream,gao2024cat3d} or structured latents encoding geometry and appearance~\cite{xiang2025trellis,xiang2026trellis2}. GenAssets learns driving-asset priors, CompleteSplat completes hidden Gaussian content, and JRM aggregates unaligned object observations through latent flow matching~\cite{yang2025genassets,liao2025complete,wu2026jrm}. LatentWorld organizes semantic occupancy generation around entity and background latents~\cite{park2026latentworld}, making structured scene generation an important point of comparison. DriveCompose targets RGB-renderable reconstruction conditioned on driving logs: a shared completion model exchanges context across world-space background and canonical vehicle and human codes. Observed codes and known free space constrain sampling, and original-view rendering checks the assembled result before reuse.

\paragraph{Generative driving scenes and videos.}
MagicDrive, DriveDreamer, and Vista generate driving imagery conditioned on scene structure or control~\cite{gao2024magicdrive,wang2024drivedreamer,gao2024vista}. StreetCrafter uses generated views to supervise dynamic Gaussian reconstruction; HorizonForge combines scene editing with video diffusion~\cite{streetcrafter,wang2026horizonforge}. WorldSplat generates Gaussians and refines their rendered videos, while DIFIX3D+ applies diffusion-based image refinement~\cite{zhu2025worldsplat,wu2025difix3d}. These methods connect generative imagery and three-dimensional scene representations at different stages. DriveCompose performs completion during scene construction and stores the resulting background and canonical assets; subsequent camera, rigid-body, and skeletal states render the same completed content.

